// Practice quiz: Running a first program
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/java-basics/running-a-first-program

::o1 run the class::You compiled Main.java and now have Main.class. Which command runs the program? {
=java Main#Correct. The java launcher takes the class name, with no .class ending.
~java Main.class#Not quite. java treats Main.class as a class name and fails with Could not find or load main class Main.class.
~javac Main#Not quite. javac compiles source files; it needs Main.java and never runs a program.
~run Main.class#Not quite. There is no run command in the JDK; the launcher is java.
}

::o1 what javac makes::What does javac Report.java produce when the code has no errors? {
=A file named Report.class containing bytecode, and no other output#Correct. javac writes bytecode next to the source and prints nothing on success.
~The program's output in the terminal#Not quite. javac compiles only; running is a separate java step.
~A native program named Report.exe#Not quite. javac writes bytecode for the Java virtual machine, not a native program.
~A changed Report.java with the errors removed#Not quite. javac never edits your source file.
}

::o2 source-file mode::What does java Hello.java do in JDK 25? {
=Compiles the file in memory and runs it, without writing Hello.class#Correct. Source-file mode compiles and runs in one step and leaves no .class file behind.
~Fails, because java only accepts compiled classes#Not quite. Since JDK 11 the launcher can run a source file directly.
~Writes Hello.class and stops without running#Not quite. That describes javac, and source-file mode writes no class file.
~Starts Maven to build the project#Not quite. The java launcher does not use Maven.
}

::o2 when the shortcut is enough::When is java Hello.java a good choice instead of a build tool? {
=Trying out a small program while learning#Correct. Source-file mode suits small programs; projects with libraries and tests use a build tool such as Maven.
~Building a project that uses libraries and a test suite#Not quite. Libraries, tests and packaging are what a build tool such as Maven manages.
~Making a file to give to users without the source#Not quite. Source-file mode writes no class file at all.
~Speeding up a large program#Not quite. It compiles every time it runs, so it is about convenience, not speed.
}

::o3 locate the error::javac prints Calc.java\:7\: error\: ';' expected. Where do you look first? {
=Calc.java, line 7, near the caret, usually the end of that statement#Correct. The message gives file and line, and a missing semicolon is reported where the statement should end.
~Line 1, because errors start at the top#Not quite. The number after the file name is the line to read.
~The 7th error in the list#Not quite. The 7 is a line number; the count of errors is printed at the end.
~The java command, because the program crashed#Not quite. This is a compile error from javac; nothing ran.
}
